'''Liyanagamage Ranganath Prabashwara Dias''', known as '''Rang Dias''', is a scientist with a primary interest in [[condensed matter physics]]. He was an [[assistant professor]] in the departments of Mechanical Engineering and Physics and Astronomy at the [[University of Rochester]] (UR), and a scientist at the UR [[Laboratory for Laser Energetics]]. As of November 19, 2024, he was no longer employed at UR.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Nidhi |first1=Subbaraman |title=Embattled Superconductivity Scientist Is Out |url=https://www.wsj.com/science/university-rochester-ranga-dias-superconductor-misconduct-61288727 |work=The Wall Street Journal |date=November 19, 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

In 2020 and in 2023, his group published two papers claiming to have achieved [[room-temperature superconductor|room-temperature superconductivity]], the first using [[carbonaceous sulfur hydride]] at extremely high pressure, and the second using [[nitrogen]]-[[doping (semiconductor)|doped]] [[lutetium]] [[hydride]] at near-ambient pressure. Both papers were later retracted after accusations of [[scientific misconduct]], including [[data fabrication]] and manipulation.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=Allegations of Scientific Misconduct Mount as Physicist Makes His Biggest Claim Yet |url=https://physics.aps.org/articles/v16/40 |access-date=March 24, 2023 |journal=Physics |date=March 9, 2023 |volume=16 |page=40 |language=en |last1=Garisto |first1=Dan |doi=10.1103/Physics.16.40 |bibcode=2023PhyOJ..16...40G |s2cid=257615348 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Superconductor Now a Reality at Room Temperature -- But Not Room Pressure |url=https://www.insidescience.org/news/superconductor-now-reality-room-temperature-not-room-pressure |access-date=March 24, 2023 |website=Inside Science |date=October 14, 2020 |language=en}}</ref> Dias denied those charges, with an initial investigation by UR in 2021 reporting no evidence of misconduct.<ref name=":5" /><ref name=":4" /> A later independent investigation performed by the [[American Physical Society]] did find such evidence,<ref name="nature.com">{{cite journal |last1=Garisto |first1=Dan |title='A very disturbing picture': another retraction imminent for controversial physicist |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-023-02401-2 |journal=Nature |date=2023 |volume=620 |issue=7972 |pages=14–16 |doi=10.1038/d41586-023-02401-2 |pmid=37491414 |bibcode=2023Natur.620...14G |s2cid=260162594 |access-date=July 26, 2023|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref name=":8">{{cite web |last1=Staff |first1=CT |title=University professor sees second paper retracted under accusations of data fabrication |url=https://www.campustimes.org/2023/07/22/university-professor-sees-second-paper-retracted-under-accusations-of-data-fabrication/ |website=Campus Times|date=July 23, 2023 |access-date=July 26, 2023}}</ref><ref name="scientificamerican.com">{{cite web |last1=Garisto |first1=Dan |title=Controversial Physicist Faces Mounting Accusations of Scientific Misconduct |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/controversial-physicist-faces-mounting-accusations-of-scientific-misconduct/ |website=Scientific American |access-date=July 29, 2023}}</ref><ref name="nytimes.com">{{cite news |last1=Chang |first1=Kenneth |title=A Looming Retraction Casts a Shadow Over a Field of Physics |work=The New York Times |date=July 26, 2023 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/26/science/ranga-dias-retraction-physics.html |access-date=July 29, 2023}}</ref> and a March 2024 investigation by the University reported that Dias "engaged in research misconduct."<ref name=":10">{{Cite journal |last=Subbaraman |first=Nidhi |date=2024-03-20 |title=Superconductor Scientist Engaged in Research Misconduct, Probe Finds |url=https://www.wsj.com/science/physics/superconductor-scientist-engaged-in-research-misconduct-probe-finds-d692898c |journal=Wall Street Journal |language=en}}</ref><ref name="garisto2024">{{cite news|last=Garisto|first=Dan|date=April 6, 2024|url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-024-00976-y|title=Exclusive: official investigation reveals how superconductivity physicist faked blockbuster results|work=Nature}}</ref>

As of 2025, Dias and his collaborator Ashkan Salamat at [[University of Nevada, Las Vegas]] have had five of their research papers retracted, and five other papers have received an Expression of Concern by the publishers.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Castelvecchi |first=Davide |date=2023-11-07 |title=Nature retracts controversial superconductivity paper by embattled physicist |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-023-03398-4 |journal=Nature |language=en |doi=10.1038/d41586-023-03398-4 |pmid=37935863 |issn=0028-0836|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref name=":11">{{cite web |title=Retraction Watch Database |url=http://retractiondatabase.org/RetractionSearch.aspx#?auth%3dDias%252c%2bRanga%2bP |website=Retraction Watch |publisher=Crossref |access-date=2025-10-03}}</ref>

Dias founded a company related to his superconductivity interests, Unearthly Materials, which made misleading claims about its funding and investors.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chant |first=Tim De |date=March 17, 2023 |title=Unearthly Materials claimed to have big-name investors, but they weren't all on board |url=https://techcrunch.com/2023/03/17/unearthly-materials-superconductors-investors/ |access-date=March 24, 2023 |website=TechCrunch |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Liyanagamage Ranganath Prabashwara Dias was born into a middle-class family in [[Kottawa]], [[Sri Lanka]].<ref name="Bresnick">{{Cite web |last=Bresnick |first=Sam |title=Brain behind Metallic Hydrogen: Pressing on Pressure |url=https://www.dailynews.lk/2017/02/08/features/107001/brain-behind-metallic-hydrogen-pressing-pressure |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=Daily News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Bio |url=https://scholar.harvard.edu/dias/bio |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=scholar.harvard.edu |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Subba |first=Sanghamitra |date=March 8, 2021 |title=UR Professor Ranga Dias has brought the world one step closer to hoverboards and magnetic levitation trains |url=https://www.campustimes.org/2021/03/07/ur-professor-ranga-dias-has-brought-the-world-one-step-closer-to-hoverboards-and-magnetic-levitation-trains/ |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=Campus Times |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":6">{{Cite web |last=Kariyawasam |first=Dilshani |date=December 8, 2020 |title=Sri Lankan-lead team discovers world's first room-temperature superconductor |url=https://colombogazette.com/2020/12/08/sri-lankan-lead-team-discovers-worlds-first-room-temperature-superconductor/ |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=Colombo Gazette |language=en-US}}</ref> He is the eldest of three children, and has a younger brother and sister. He completed elementary school at the [[St. Joseph's Boys' College, Nugegoda]], middle school at the [[President's College, Maharagama]], and high school studies at the [[Royal College, Colombo]].<ref name=":6" />

He received his B.S. in physics from the [[University of Colombo]] in 2006.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ORCID |url=https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0830-4928 |access-date=March 24, 2023 |website=orcid.org}}</ref> In 2013, he obtained his Ph.D. in physics from [[Washington State University]], where he studied under Professor Choong-shik Yoo.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wolcott |first=R.J. |date=March 12, 2021 |title=WSU graduate recognized as Time100 Next innovator |url=https://news.wsu.edu/news/2021/03/12/wsu-graduate-recognized-time100-next-innovator/ |access-date=March 24, 2023 |website=WSU Insider |publisher=WSU News |language=en-US}}</ref> After his Ph.D., he was hired by Professor Isaac Silvera at the Department of Physics, [[Harvard University]], to research [[metallic hydrogen]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Harvard Scholar Bio |url=https://scholar.harvard.edu/dias/bio |access-date=March 24, 2023 |website=scholar.harvard.edu |language=en}}</ref><ref name="Bresnick"/>

In January 2017, together with Silvera, Dias (then a postdoctoral fellow at [[Harvard University]]) reported the creation of solid [[metallic hydrogen]] using a [[diamond anvil cell]].<ref name="HG">{{Cite news |date=2017-01-26 |title=Advance in high-pressure physics |language=en-US |work=Harvard Gazette |url=https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/2017/01/a-breakthrough-in-high-pressure-physics/ |access-date=2018-01-04}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Amos|first=Jonathan |date=2017 |title=Hydrogen 'wonder material' claim made |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/science-environment-38768683|access-date=2018-01-04}}</ref> This state of hydrogen was originally predicted by [[Eugene Wigner]] and [[Hillard Bell Huntington]] in 1935,<ref>{{cite web |last1=Flores |first1=Felipe |title=The Discovery of Metallic Hydrogen – Harvard Science Review |url=https://harvardsciencereview.com/2017/05/16/the-discovery-of-metallic-hydrogen/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171007112652/https://harvardsciencereview.com/2017/05/16/the-discovery-of-metallic-hydrogen/ |url-status=usurped |archive-date=October 7, 2017 |website=harvardsciencereview.com |access-date=4 March 2019}}</ref> since which scientists have been trying to make the substance. However, several physicists expressed skepticism about the result.<ref name="Castelvecchi 17–17">{{Cite journal|last=Castelvecchi|first=Davide|date=2017-02-02|title=Physicists doubt bold report of metallic hydrogen |journal=Nature |language=en |volume=542 |issue=7639 |page=17 |doi=10.1038/nature.2017.21379 |pmid=28150796 |bibcode=2017Natur.542...17C |doi-access=free}}</ref>

== Career ==
In 2017, Dias became an assistant professor in both the Department of Mechanical Engineering and the Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Rochester.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Faculty 2017–2018 |url=https://www.rochester.edu/college/ourfaculty/new1718.html |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=www.rochester.edu |language=en-US |archive-date=April 3, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230403051926/https://www.rochester.edu/college/ourfaculty/new1718.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Dias's research focuses on properties of materials at extreme pressure and temperature conditions. These materials exhibit fundamental changes in their properties, potentially leading to the discovery of novel phenomena and exotic states of matter that do not exist under normal conditions. He is particularly interested in [[high-temperature superconductivity]] and [[quantum phase transitions]] of [[hydrogen]] and [[hydride]] materials.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Silvera |first1=Isaac F. |last2=Dias |first2=Ranga |date=January 1, 2021 |title=Phases of the hydrogen isotopes under pressure: metallic hydrogen |journal=Advances in Physics: X |volume=6 |issue=1 |article-number=1961607 |doi=10.1080/23746149.2021.1961607|bibcode=2021AdPhX...661607S |s2cid=238638324 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name="Snider"/><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dias |first1=Ranga P. |last2=Silvera |first2=Isaac F. |date=February 17, 2017 |title=Observation of the Wigner-Huntington transition to metallic hydrogen |url=https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aal1579 |journal=Science |language=en |volume=355 |issue=6326 |pages=715–718 |doi=10.1126/science.aal1579 |pmid=28126728 |arxiv=1610.01634 |bibcode=2017Sci...355..715D |s2cid=52851498 |issn=0036-8075}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dias |first1=Ranga P. |last2=Noked |first2=Ori |last3=Silvera |first3=Isaac F. |date=April 8, 2016 |title=New Phases and Dissociation-Recombination of Hydrogen Deuteride to 3.4 Mbar |url=https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevLett.116.145501 |journal=Physical Review Letters |volume=116 |issue=14 |article-number=145501 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.116.145501|pmid=27104717 |arxiv=1510.05686 |bibcode=2016PhRvL.116n5501D |s2cid=115503 }}</ref><ref name=":7">{{Cite journal |last1=Dasenbrock-Gammon |first1=Nathan |last2=Snider |first2=Elliot |last3=McBride |first3=Raymond |last4=Pasan |first4=Hiranya |last5=Durkee |first5=Dylan |last6=Khalvashi-Sutter |first6=Nugzari |last7=Munasinghe |first7=Sasanka |last8=Dissanayake |first8=Sachith E. |last9=Lawler |first9=Keith V. |last10=Salamat |first10=Ashkan |last11=Dias |first11=Ranga P. |date=March 1, 2023 |title=Evidence of near-ambient superconductivity in a N-doped lutetium hydride |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-05742-0 |journal=Nature |language=en |volume=615 |issue=7951 |pages=244–250 |doi=10.1038/s41586-023-05742-0 |pmid=36890373 |bibcode=2023Natur.615..244D |s2cid=257407449 |issn=1476-4687|url-access=subscription }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1038/s41586-023-06774-2|pmid=37935926|intentional=yes}}</ref> His research utilizes a [[diamond anvil cell]] and [[lasers]] to achieve extreme pressure and temperatures in materials.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dasenbrock-Gammon |first1=Nathan |last2=McBride |first2=Raymond |last3=Yoo |first3=Gyeongjae |last4=Dissanayake |first4=Sachith |last5=Dias |first5=Ranga |date=September 1, 2022 |title=Second harmonic AC calorimetry technique within a diamond anvil cell |url=https://aip.scitation.org/doi/10.1063/5.0104705 |journal=Review of Scientific Instruments |volume=93 |issue=9 |page=093901 |doi=10.1063/5.0104705 |pmid=36182513 |arxiv=2206.10072 |bibcode=2022RScI...93i3901D |s2cid=249889303 |issn=0034-6748}}</ref>

In 2021, he was named as an innovation leader in the [[Time 100|''Time'' 100 Next]] list.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wolcott |first=R. J. |title=WSU graduate recognized as Time100 Next innovator |url=https://news.wsu.edu/news/2021/03/12/wsu-graduate-recognized-time100-next-innovator/ |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=WSU Insider |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |date=February 17, 2021 |title=2021 TIME100 Next: Ranga Dias |url=https://time.com/collection/time100-next-2021/5937727/ranga-dias/ |access-date=April 3, 2023 |magazine=Time |language=en}}</ref>

As of November 19, 2024, he was no longer employed at the University of Rochester.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Nidhi |first1=Subbaraman |title=Embattled Superconductivity Scientist Is Out |url=https://www.wsj.com/science/university-rochester-ranga-dias-superconductor-misconduct-61288727 |work=The Wall Street Journal|date=November 19, 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

== Scientific misconduct investigations ==
As of 2024, five of Dias's papers have been retracted, all with a small cohort of coauthors.<ref name=":11" /> An extensive Research Misconduct Investigation, conducted by the University of Rochester at the request of the [[National Science Foundation]], uncovered multiple evidence for Research Misconduct, including verbatim text and figure plagiarism in a NSF grant application and multiple instances of data falsification and fabrication. A Nature News article revealed that while the report was initially confidential, it became public in court documents.<ref name="garisto2024"/>

=== Published peer-reviewed works ===
Dias's research into room temperature superconductivity and [[metallic hydrogen]] has been the subject of controversy and scrutiny due to concerns regarding data availability, data analysis methods, and lack of [[reproducibility]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=van der Marel |first1=Dirk |last2=Hirsch |first2=Jorge E. |date=January 19, 2022 |title=Comment on Nature 586, 373 (2020) by E. Snider et al |journal=International Journal of Modern Physics B |volume=37 |issue=4 |page=2375001 |arxiv=2201.07686 |doi=10.1142/S0217979223750012 |issn=0217-9792 |s2cid=252324362}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Bushwick |first=Sophie |title=Controversy Surrounds Blockbuster Superconductivity Claim |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/will-controversy-sink-this-superconductivity-breakthrough/ |access-date=March 24, 2023 |website=Scientific American |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title='Revolutionary' blue crystal resurrects hope of room temperature superconductivity |url=https://www.science.org/content/article/revolutionary-blue-crystal-resurrects-hope-room-temperature-superconductivity |access-date=March 24, 2023 |website=www.science.org |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite news |last=Wood |first=Charlie |title=Room-Temperature Superconductor Discovery Meets With Resistance |work=[[Quanta Magazine]] |url=https://www.quantamagazine.org/room-temperature-superconductor-discovery-meets-with-resistance-20230308/ |access-date=2023-11-10}}</ref><ref name="Garisto 40">{{Cite journal |last=Garisto |first=Dan |date=March 9, 2023 |title=Allegations of Scientific Misconduct Mount as Physicist Makes His Biggest Claim Yet |url=https://physics.aps.org/articles/v16/40 |journal=Physics |language=en |volume=16 |page=40|doi=10.1103/Physics.16.40 |bibcode=2023PhyOJ..16...40G |s2cid=257615348 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

In 2020, Dias published a paper in ''[[Nature (journal)|Nature]]'' describing room-temperature superconductivity in a [[carbonaceous sulfur hydride]].<ref name="Snider">{{cite journal |last1=Snider |first1=Elliot |last2=Dasenbrock-Gammon |first2=Nathan |last3=McBride |first3=Raymond |last4=Debessai |first4=Mathew |last5=Vindana |first5=Hiranya |last6=Vencatasamy |first6=Kevin |last7=Lawler |first7=Keith V. |last8=Salamat |first8=Ashkan |last9=Dias |first9=Ranga P. |title=Room-temperature superconductivity in a carbonaceous sulfur hydride |journal=Nature |date=October 15, 2020 |volume=586 |issue=7829 |pages=373–377 |doi=10.1038/s41586-020-2801-z|pmid=33057222 |bibcode=2020Natur.586..373S |osti=1673473 |s2cid=222823227 }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1038/s41586-022-05294-9|pmid=36163290|intentional=yes}}</ref><ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last=Bushwick |first=Sophie |title=Controversy Surrounds Blockbuster Superconductivity Claim |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/will-controversy-sink-this-superconductivity-breakthrough/ |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=Scientific American |language=en}}</ref> When asked about a potential [[Nobel Prize]] for this discovery, Dias said: "Yes, this has a potential for such high recognition, but I do not believe this will happen in the near future."<ref name=":6"/> It was retracted by ''Nature'' in 2022. Condensed matter theorist [[Jorge E. Hirsch]] was the first to question peculiarities in the published data.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hirsch |first1=Jorge E. |date=September 26, 2021 |title=On the ac magnetic susceptibility of a room temperature superconductor: anatomy of a probable scientific fraud |journal=Physica C: Superconductivity and Its Applications |volume=613 |article-number=1354228 |arxiv=2110.12854v1 |doi=10.1016/j.physc.2023.1354228 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Preprint server removes 'inflammatory' papers in superconductor controversy |url=https://www.science.org/content/article/preprint-server-removes-inflammatory-papers-superconductor-controversy |access-date=March 14, 2022 |website=www.science.org |language=en}}</ref> [[Marvin L. Cohen]], [[National Medal of Science]] recipient and former president of the [[American Physical Society]], was also an early voice pointing out problems with the carbonaceous sulfur hydride paper.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Dogan |first1=Mehmet |last2=Cohen |first2=Marvin |title=Anomalous behavior in high-pressure carbonaceous sulfur hydride |journal=Physica C |date=2021 |volume=583 |issue=15 |article-number=1353851 |doi=10.1016/j.physc.2021.1353851 |bibcode=2021PhyC..58353851D |s2cid=229340504 |arxiv=2012.10771 }}</ref> 

After the paper was retracted, Dias and co-author Ashkan Salamat denied accusations of misconduct. Initial investigations by the University of Rochester found no evidence to support the accusations,<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Magazine |first=Undark |date=March 27, 2023 |title=A Potential Triumph in Physics, Dogged by Accusation and Doubt |url=https://undark.org/2023/03/27/a-potential-triumph-in-physics-dogged-by-accusation-and-doubt/ |access-date=April 3, 2023 |website=Undark Magazine |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":4" /> but in 2024 they found Dias had engaged in research misconduct.<ref name=":10" /> 

In 2022, James Hamlin, a physicist at the [[University of Florida]], alleged that Dias may have copied data in a 2021 paper from an experiment on a different material in his 2013 Ph.D. thesis.<ref>{{Cite journal |title='A very disturbing picture': another retraction imminent for controversial physicist |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-023-02401-2 |date=July 25, 2023 |access-date=July 26, 2023 |journal=Nature |doi=10.1038/d41586-023-02401-2 |language=en |last1=Garisto |first1=Dan |volume=620 |issue=7972 |pages=14–16 |pmid=37491414 |bibcode=2023Natur.620...14G |s2cid=260162594 |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Coauthor Ashkan Salamat denied accusations of wrongdoing in an interview.<ref name=":4" /> However, the journal ''[[Physical Review Letters]]'' subsequently added an 'expression of concern' to the paper, and is conducting an investigation.<ref name="Garisto 40"/> Hamlin discovered the similarity between the figures while following up on his discovery that Dias had plagiarized part of that thesis from Hamlin's own Ph.D. thesis.<ref name=":12">{{Cite web |title=Plagiarism allegations pursue physicist behind stunning superconductivity claims |url=https://www.science.org/content/article/plagiarism-allegations-pursue-physicist-behind-stunning-superconductivity-claims |access-date=April 13, 2023 |website=www.science.org |language=en}}</ref>

In April 2023, a comment on another of Dias's papers in the journal ''[[ChemComm]]'' (which was said to be a partial replication of his CSH superconductivity results) was published.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hirsch |first=Jorge  |date=April 21, 2023 |title=Comment on "Carbon content drives high temperature superconductivity in a carbonaceous sulfur hydride below 100 GPa" by G. A. Smith, I. E. Collings, E. Snider, D. Smith, S. Petitgirard, J. S. Smith, M. White, E. Jones, P. Ellison, K. V. Lawler, R. P. Dias and A. Salamat, Chem. Commun., 2022, 59, 5765–5770 |journal=Chemical Communications |volume=59 |issue=38 |language=en |pages=5765–5770  |doi=10.1039/D2CC05277F |pmid=37083704 }}</ref> The comment says that:

{{Blockquote
|text="with probability larger than {{nowrap|1–10<sup>−338</sup>}} some of those data were not measured in a laboratory, contrary to what the papers claim. This finding undermines confidence in the claim that ''any'' of the experimental evidence reported in those papers reflects the properties of real physical samples of CSH."
}}

On July 25, 2023, it was announced that a 2021 paper in ''Physical Review Letters'' (''PRL'') on which Dias was a co-author would be retracted due to suspected data fabrication. The journal commissioned an investigation by four independent referees and concluded that "The findings back up the allegations of data fabrication/falsification convincingly". Two of the referees reported on "a very disturbing picture of apparent data fabrication followed by an attempt to hide or coverup [sic] the fact. We urge immediate retraction of the paper".<ref name="nature.com"/><ref name=":8"/><ref name="scientificamerican.com"/><ref name="nytimes.com"/> The ''PRL'' retraction said that "Dias stands by the data in Fig. 1(b) and does not agree to retract the Letter".<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Durkee |first1=Dylan |last2=Dasenbrock-Gammon |first2=Nathan |last3=Smith |first3=G. Alexander |last4=Snider |first4=Elliot |last5=Smith |first5=Dean |last6=Childs |first6=Christian |last7=Kimber |first7=Simon A. J. |last8=Lawler |first8=Keith V. |last9=Salamat |first9=Ashkan |date=2023-08-15 |title=Retraction: Colossal Density-Driven Resistance Response in the Negative Charge Transfer Insulator <math>MnS_2</math> [Phys. Rev. Lett. 127, 016401 (2021)] |journal=Physical Review Letters |volume=131 |issue=7 |article-number=079902 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.131.079902|pmid=37656869 |doi-access=free }}</ref> He was the only author to do so, and all other authors agreed to the retraction. He responded that some errors in charts were unintentionally introduced in [[Adobe Illustrator]] by his colleagues. One of the reviewers hired by ''PRL'' to conduct an independent review said Dias's response was "both inadequate and disappointing", adding that Adobe Illustrator was never mentioned in the discussions among the authors, Hamlin, and ''PRL''{{'}}s editors that were provided to the reviewers. The University of Rochester spokesperson said the university "has a comprehensive investigation underway into the questions raised about the integrity of all data at issue in this and other studies".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Chang |first1=Kenneth |title=Superconductor Scientist Faces Investigation as a Paper Is Retracted |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/08/15/science/retraction-ranga-dias-rochester.html |work=The New York Times |date=August 15, 2023 |access-date=2023-11-10 |language=en}}</ref>

On September 26, 2023, ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'' reported that the coauthors of the N-doped lutetium hydride ''Nature'' paper<ref name=":7" /> were requesting a retraction, alleging Dias of misrepresenting data. He denied the allegation and said he did not agree to retract.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Subbaraman |first=Nidhi |title=WSJ News Exclusive {{!}} Co-Authors Seek to Retract Paper Claiming Superconductor Breakthrough |url=https://www.wsj.com/science/room-temperature-superconductor-retract-journal-nature-e554536a |access-date=2023-09-27 |website=WSJ |language=en-US}}</ref>  On November 7, 2023 ''Nature'' formally retracted the paper in question,<ref name=":7" /><ref name=":9">{{Cite journal |last1=Dasenbrock-Gammon |first1=Nathan |last2=Snider |first2=Elliot |last3=McBride |first3=Raymond |last4=Pasan |first4=Hiranya |last5=Durkee |first5=Dylan |last6=Khalvashi-Sutter |first6=Nugzari |last7=Munasinghe |first7=Sasanka |last8=Dissanayake |first8=Sachith E. |last9=Lawler |first9=Keith V. |last10=Salamat |first10=Ashkan |last11=Dias |first11=Ranga P. |date=2023-11-07 |title=Retraction Note: Evidence of near-ambient superconductivity in a N-doped lutetium hydride |journal=Nature |volume=624 |issue=7991 |page=460 |language=en |doi=10.1038/s41586-023-06774-2 |issn=1476-4687|doi-access=free |pmid=37935926 |bibcode=2023Natur.624..460D }}</ref> with the statement indicating that "... request of the authors Nathan Dasenbrock-Gammon, Elliot Snider, Raymond McBride, Hiranya Pasan, Dylan Durkee, Sachith E. Dissanayake, Keith V. Lawler and Ashkan Salamat." The retraction note states that the remaining authors "Nugzari Khalvashi-Sutter, Sasanka Munasinghe and Ranga P. Dias have not stated whether they agree or disagree with this retraction."<ref name=":9" />

Commenting in ''[[Physics World]]'' about Dias and the controversy surrounding his superconductivity work, physicist Lilia Boeri said his "inconsiderate behaviour has harmed the reputation of the field". James Hamlin said, "I do think the whole saga is damaging to science in general, and superconductivity research more so, and more broadly it's fuel for anti-science types."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Banks |first1=Michael |title=Superconductivity 'damaged' as researchers look to move on from retractions |url=https://physicsworld.com/a/superconductivity-damaged-as-researchers-look-to-move-on-from-retractions/ |website=Physics World |date=October 20, 2023 |publisher=IOP Publishing |access-date=2023-10-25}}</ref>

In 2024, a news report in ''[[Nature (magazine)|Nature]]'' noted that " Dias is now infamous for the scandal that surrounds his work," that "many other research groups have tried and failed to replicate Dias's superconductivity results," revealed that the University of Rochester had "launched a fourth investigation, led by experts external to the university [following which] Dias was stripped of his students and laboratories," and "the evidence raises questions about why the problems in Dias's lab did not prompt stronger action, and sooner, by his collaborators, by Nature's journal team and by his university."<ref name="Nature news 2024">{{cite news |last1=Garisto |first1=Dan |title=Superconductivity scandal: the inside story of deception in a rising star's physics lab |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-024-00716-2 |access-date=2024-03-18 |journal=Nature |date=2024-03-08 |doi=10.1038/d41586-024-00716-2}}</ref>

=== Unearthly Materials ===
In 2020, Dias and Salamat formed a start up company called Unearthly Materials, intended to commercially exploit patents held by Dias and the [[University of Rochester]] related to Dias' alleged discoveries of high temperature superconductivity.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Marcotte |first1=Bob |title=Room temperature superconductor? Rochester lab sets new record toward long-sought goal |url=https://www.rochester.edu/newscenter/rochester-sets-new-record-for-room-temperature-superconductor-455722/ |website=The University of Rochester |access-date=Aug 4, 2023}}</ref>

In March 2023, a reporter for ''[[Quanta Magazine]]'' found a 2021 YouTube talk in which Dias claimed that Unearthly Materials had raised $20 million and listed investors that included the CEOs of [[OpenAI]] and [[Spotify]]. A representative for Dias then retracted the claims of both the funding and the investors, saying they were "aspirational".<ref name=":5" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=C. T. |date=2023-07-23 |title=Professor faces second paper retraction due to alleged data manipulation |url=https://www.campustimes.org/2023/07/22/university-professor-sees-second-paper-retracted-under-accusations-of-data-fabrication/ |access-date=2023-10-04 |website=Campus Times |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== 2013, Washington State University, Ph.D. thesis ===
In April 2023, the journal ''[[Science (journal)|Science]]'' reported at least 21% of Dias's 2013 doctoral thesis, supervised at Washington State University, had been identified as copied from uncredited sources, including the 2007 doctoral thesis of James Hamlin, at Washington University in St. Louis, in addition to parts of a 1999 paper of Dias's PhD adviser, Choong-Shik Yoo.<ref>{{Cite news |last=GARISTO |first=Daniel |date=13 April 2023 |title=Plagiarism allegations pursue physicist behind stunning superconductivity claims - Evidence suggests much of Ranga Dias's thesis was copied from other sources |work=Science |url=https://www.science.org/content/article/plagiarism-allegations-pursue-physicist-behind-stunning-superconductivity-claims |access-date=2023-11-10}}</ref><ref name=":8" /> Analysis of Dias's thesis by Lisa Rasmussen, a research ethicist at the [[University of North Carolina at Charlotte|University of North Carolina, Charlotte]], also indicated plagiarism.<ref name=":12" />

